==Latin==

===Etymology===
{{etymon|la|id=the Americas|der|it>Amerigo>personal name}}
Feminine form of ''Americus'', the Latinized form of the forename of Italian explorer [[:w:Amerigo Vespucci|Amerigo Vespucci]] (1454–1512).  {{m|it|Amerigo}} is the {{der|la|it|-}} form of a {{der|la|gem|-}} personal name (see {{m|en|Emmerich}}).

First recorded in 1507 (together with the related term ''Amerigen'') in the ''{{w|Cosmographiae Introductio}}'', apparently written by [[w:Matthias Ringmann|Matthias Ringmann]], in reference to South America;<ref>John R. Hebert, "The Map That Named America: Library Acquires 1507 Waldseemüller Map of the World" ([http://www.loc.gov/loc/lcib/0309/maps.html]), ''Information Bulletin'', Library of Congress</ref> first applied to both North and South America by Mercator in 1538. ''Amerigen'' means "land of Amerigo" and derives from ''Amerigo'' and ''[[gen]]'', the accusative case of Greek ''[[γῆ|gē]]'' "earth". ''America'' accorded with the feminine names of [[Asia]], [[Africa]], and [[Europa]].<ref name=Smiths>Toby Lester, "Putting America on the Map", ''Smithsonian'', 40:9 (December 2009)</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|Amerī̆ca}}
* Note: the length of the /i/ can be long, and that form is well-attested in Latin literature; for example, {{w|Rafael Landívar|Rafael Landívar's|lang=es}} Rusticatio Mexicana. This form is mostly attested in poetry, and it corresponds more closely to the Italian pronunciation of {{m|it|Amerigo}}.<ref>Gil, J. (2010). "America. Myrtia, 25, 187–194." ([https://revistas.um.es/myrtia/article/view/131651/122081])</ref>

===Proper noun===
