{{also|Amerika|Ameríka|Amerîka|Amerikā|Ameriką|Amẹrika|amerika}}
{{minitoc}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{bor|en|de|Amerika}}, in imitation of the German-speaking [[Nazi]]s.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/əˈmɛ.ɹɪ.kə/}}

====Proper noun====
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{lb|en|pejorative|political}} [[America]]; {{non-gloss|used to imply that the US is [[fascist]].}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1971|author=w:Abbie Hoffman|title=w:Steal This Book|url=https://archive.org/details/stealthisbook00hoff/page/n3/mode/2up?q=Amerika|page=4|passage=Steal This Book is, in a way, a manual of survival in the prison that is '''Amerika'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2008-04-30|author=Zoe Trodd|title=American Protest Literature|publisher=Harvard University Press|isbn=9780674027633|page=444
|text=CARL WITTMAN From ''Refugees from '''Amerika''': A Gay Manifesto'' 1969 <br/>If the gay liberation movement began on the East Coast with the Stonewall riots, it began on the West Coast with the publication of "Refugees from '''Amerika'''."}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{col|en|Amerika Bay|Gulf of Amerika}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|Amerikkka}}

===Etymology 2===

====Proper noun====
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{given name|en|female}}, {{n-g|a variant of {{m|en|America}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Sam Warren|title=Tales from the Tijuana Jails|publisher=Bookwarren Publishing Servi|isbn=9780945949572|page=203
|text={{...}} As far as he could determine, the victim had been stealing from '''Amerika''' in order to support his crystal habit. The victim had also been Amerika's lover and had lived in the ''carraca'' with her.}}

{{c|en|United States}}

