==Japanese==
{{wikipedia|lang=ja}}
[[Image:LocationEurope.png|thumb|World map showing the location of Europe (continent).]]
{{ja-kanjitab|alt=欧羅巴:obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{etymon|ja|der|pt>Europa>Europe|id=Europe}}
{{IPAchar|/europpa/}} {{IPAchar|[jeuɾoppa]}} → {{IPAchar|/joːroppa/}}

From {{der|ja|pt|Europa}}.<ref>{{R:Kokugo Dai Jiten}}</ref><ref>{{R:Daijisen}}</ref><ref name="KJE">{{R:Kojien}}</ref><ref>{{R:Daijirin}}</ref><ref name="NHK">{{R:NHK Hatsuon}}</ref>

Some references<ref name="KJE"/> source this from {{der|ja|nl|Europa}}; however, Dutch phonology makes this less likely, as the {{IPAchar|⟨eu⟩}} vowel combination has apparently been pronounced as the [[monophthong]]s {{IPAchar|/œː/}} or {{IPAchar|/øː/}} since the time of {{w|Middle Dutch}}.  Meanwhile, in Portuguese, the ⟨eu⟩ vowel combination has been pronounced as the [[diphthong]] {{IPAchar|/ew/}}, a closer match for the borrowed Japanese pronunciation.

Although this term is a {{m|ja|外来語|tr=gairaigo|t=word of non-native or non-[[Middle Chinese]] origin}}, it was borrowed early enough to be subject to the sound change from {{IPAchar|/eu/}} to {{IPAchar|/joː/}} during {{w|Late Middle Japanese}}. Compare similar developments in English ({{IPAchar|/ɛu̯/}} → {{IPAchar|/juː/}}; {{IPAchar|/jʊ/}}, {{IPAchar|/jɔː/}}, {{IPAchar|/jɜː/}} before {{IPAchar|/r/}}) during the {{w|Great Vowel Shift}}, which yields the modern English pronunciation of {{m|en|Europe}}.

Alternatively, it is merely a {{w|historical kana orthography}}-induced {{spelling pronunciation|ja|nocap=y}}; compare {{ja-r|要|えう|rom=yō}}, as well as {{ja-r|アルコール}}.

{{doublet|ja|エウローペー|tr1=Eurōpē|t1=[[Europa]]}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{ja-pron|acc=3|acc_ref=SMK5,NHK}}

===Proper noun===
{{ja-pos|proper|hkata=エウロツパ}}

# [[Europe]] {{gl|continent}}
#: {{syn|ja|欧州|tr=Ōshū}}

