==Latin==
{{wikipedia|lang=la}}

===Etymology===
Feminine of {{m|la|āfricus}}/{{m|la|Āfricus}}, as a noun elliptic of {{m|la|[[terra]] āfrica/Āfrica|lit=the land of the Afri}}. The adjective ''Āfricus'' comes from the name of the ''Āfrī'' (singular {{m|la|Āfer}}), a tribal people of the area near Carthage, by addition of the suffix {{m|la|-icus}}. The Latin term formed alongside Greek {{m|grc|ἡ [[Ἀφρική]]}}, both terms being attested since the first century.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|Āfrica|eccl=1}}

===Proper noun===
{{la-proper noun|Āfrica<1.loc>}}

# Northwestern Africa, the territory of [[Carthage]], the African coast west of the Nile
#: ''Nilus Africam ab Aethiopiā dispescens'' (Pliny 5, 9, 10, § 53)
# {{place|la|province|empire/Roman Empire|t=Africa}} (later split into ''Africa Zeugitana'' and ''Africa Byzacena'' under Diocletian)
# [[#English|Africa]] as a continent, understood as the quarter of the globe south of the Mediterranean
#: {{ux|la|Si probare possemus Ligarium in '''Āfricā''' omnino non fuisse.|If we could prove that Ligarius was not at all in '''Africa'''.}}

====Declension====
{{la-ndecl|Āfrica<1>}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|la|āfricānus}}
* {{l|la|āfricus}}

