Page 679 Ulimaroa: -------- begin text --------
==Swedish==
{{wikipedia|lang=sv}}
[[File:Karta över Polynesien eller femte delen af jordklotet, 1780.jpg|thumb|A map from 1780 by cartographer Daniel Djurberg.]]

===Etymology===
{{coinage|sv|Daniel Djurberg (geographer)|w=sv:Daniel Djurberg (geograf)|alt=Daniel Djurberg |in=1776 |nat=Swedish|occ=geographer |nobycat=1}} in his ''Geografi, Sammandragen utur de Nyste och Tilförliteligaste Auctorer'' (‘Geography, Summaries of the Newest and Most Comprehensive Authors'), adapted from a (by Djurberg) alleged Māori word for Australia, ''Olhemaroa'', which is mentioned in {{w|John Hawkesworth (book editor)|John Hawkesworth}}'s 1773 rendition of {{w|James Cook}}'s {{w|First voyage of James Cook|first voyage}} (1768–1771).

The ultimate origin is uncertain, but the origin of ''Ulimaroa'' is most likely {{der|sv|ty|'o}}, the Tahitian article for proper names + {{der|sv|mi|Rimaroa|t=long arm/hand}}, referring to a long narrow island, most likely to {{w|Grande Terre (New Caledonia)|Grand Terre}} of {{w|New Caledonia}}, from {{der|sv|mi|poz-pol-pro|*rima}} (< {{der|sv|map-pro|*lima}}) + {{der|sv|mi|roa|t=long}} (< {{der|sv|poz-pol-pro|*loa}}), cf. the ending in e.g. {{m|mi|Aotearoa|t=long white cloud, New Zealand}}.

The word was transmitted to the English via the Tahitian-speaking priest, chief and navigator Tupa'ia from {{w|Raiatea}} in Tahiti, hence the Tahitian article. 18th century proper names from Tahitian were commonly borrowed into English with the article, cf. {{m|en|Otaheite}} (< {{m|ty|'o}} {{m|ty|Tahiti}}) and {{m|en|Ulietea|t=Raiatea}} (< {{m|ty|'o}} {{m|ty|Ra'iatea}}).

===Proper noun===
{{sv-proper noun|n}}

# {{lb|sv|obsolete|rare}} [[Australia]]
#: {{syn|sv|Nya Holland|q1=obsolete|Australien|q2=modern}}
#* {{quote-book|sv|year=1817 |author=w:Carl Jonas Love Almqvist |title=Parjumouf. Saga ifrån Nya Holland |text=[…] en besynnerlig rysning öfverföll mig, då jag såg de ödsliga, ofantliga Sandkusterne af det stora '''Ulimaroa'''. |t=I was overwhelmed by a peculiar shiver, when I saw the desolate, immense sandy shores of the great '''Australia'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|sv|year=1834 |author=w:sv:Fredrik Cederborgh |title=Ungdoms tidsfördrif |url=https://runeberg.org/ungdomstid/3/0197.html |text=så äro, till exempel, svanorna svarta på '''Ulimaroa''', då de hos oss äro hvita |t=so are, for instance, the swans black in '''Australia''', while they are white over here.}}

===References===
* Jan Tent & Paul Geraghty (2012) ''Where in the World is Ulimaroa?'', The Journal of Pacific History, 47:1, 1-20, DOI: 10.1080/00223344.2011.647396

{{C|sv|Australia}}
