Page 694 Antarctica: -------- begin text --------
{{also|antarctica|antárctica}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}
{{wikivoyage}}
[[Image:Antarctica_satellite_globe.jpg|thumb|Antarctica in a satellite photo]]

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|NL.|Antarctica}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἀνταρκτικός}}, from {{m|grc|ἀντι-|t=anti-, against, opposed}} + {{m|grc|ἀρκτικός|t=Arctic}}, from {{m|grc|ἄρκτος|t=bear}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌænˈtɑː(k)tɪkə/|/ˌænˈɑːtɪkə/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ænˈtɑɹ(k)tɪkə/|[ɛəˈɾ̃ɑɹɾɪkə]|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/enˈtɐːktɪkə/|a=AU}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-Antarctica.ogg|a=US}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-prop}}

# {{senseid|en|Q51}}The [[southernmost]] [[continent]], south of the [[Southern Ocean]], containing the [[South Pole]].
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1891
|first=Hugh Robert
|last=Mill
|authorlink=Hugh Robert Mill
|chapter=THE CONTINENTAL AREA
|title=The Realm of Nature: An Outline of Physiography
|url=https://archive.org/details/realmofnatureout00millrich/
|location=London
|publisher=[[w:John Murray (publishing house)|John Murray]]
|year_published=1892
|OCLC=30341185
|page=274
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/realmofnatureout00millrich/page/274/
|text=The five largest islands or peninsulas in which the crests of the World Ridges break through the uniform covering of the hydrosphere are termed continents, and designated by the names Eurasia, Africa, North America, South America, and Australia. They are distinguished from other islands and peninsulas by size alone, Australia being ten times larger than New Guinea, and Africa ten times larger than Arabia, these being the greatest island and peninsula not called continents. The elevated region round the South Pole is crowned by the unexplored and scarcely discovered continent of '''Antarctica'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|year=1894
|month=August
|first=Cyrus C.
|last=Adams
|title=Antarctic Exploration
|journal=w:The American Naturalist
|url=https://archive.org/details/mobot31753002158118/page/n96/
|volume=XXVIII
|issue=332
|source=w:New York Sun
|issn=0003-0147
|oclc=45446849
|page=696
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/mobot31753002158118/page/696/
|text=Dr. Murray believes that Alexander I. Land is a part of the west coast of Graham's Land, and that this landmass, which Biscoe and Larsen proved to widen rapidly toward the south, is only a peninsula of the continent of '''Antarctica'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1988-04-14|author=Richard Fifield|title=Frozen assets of the ice cores|work=New Scientist|issue=1608|page=28|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8CLAlJR6kuEC&pg=PA28&dq=%22glaciologists%22&hl=&cd=2&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22glaciologists%22&f=false|text=To most people, the huge ice sheets  of '''Antarctica''' and Greenland are merely water that once was snow. To glaciologists and climatologists, they are storehouses of the Earth's former atmospheres.}}
# {{q|loosely}} the [[Antarctic]]

