Page 804 Polynesia: -------- begin text --------
==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|Polynésie}}, coined by {{w|Charles de Brosses}} in 1756, from {{der|en|grc|πολύς|t=many}} + {{m|grc|νῆσος|t=island}}.<ref>{{R:Etymonline|accessdate=January 21, 2020}}</ref> According to ''Moon Handbooks Tahiti'', de Brosses first used the term to refer to all Pacific islands.{{attn|en|Can we find a better source?}} The present restricted use was established by {{w|Jules Dumont d'Urville|J. S. C. Dumont d'Urville}} in 1832.<ref>{{cite-journal|author=Geoffery Clark|title=Dumont d'Urville's Oceania|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/25169636|journal=The Journal of Pacific History|volume=38|number=2|publisher=Taylor & Francis|month=September|year=2003|pages=155-161}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pɒləˈniːzi.ə/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-Polynesia.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌpɑːləˈniːʒə/|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːʒə|s=4}}

===Proper noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{place|en|A <<region>> of <<cont/Oceania>>, including [[Easter Island]], [[Hawaii]], [[New Zealand]], and most of the islands between them}}.

====Holonyms====
* {{l|en|Austronesia}}
* {{l|en|Oceania}}

====Coordinate terms====
* {{l|en|Melanesia}}
* {{l|en|Micronesia}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|Polynesian}}

