Page 830 Melanesia: -------- begin text --------
{{also|melanesia|Melanésia}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fr|Mélanésie}} (coined by {{w|Jules Dumont d'Urville}}), from {{der|en|grc|μέλας||dark}} + {{m|grc|νῆσος||island}}, referring to the skin color of the inhabitants.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌmɛl.əˈniː.zi.ə/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-Melanesia.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌmɛl.əˈni.ʒə/|a=GA}}
* {{a|en|GA}} {{rhymes|en|iːʒə|s=4}}
* {{hyphenation|en|Mel|a|ne|sia}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{place|en|A <<region>> of <<cont/Oceania>>, made up of [[New Guinea]], the [[Bismarck Archipelago]], the [[Solomon Islands]], [[New Caledonia]], [[Vanuatu]] and [[Fiji]]}}.
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|passage=My job, then, is that of a cultural and religious broker of sorts, co-opting '''Melanesia''' to serve as a stimulus to thought in Le Moyne classrooms.
|page=315
|title=Thinking and teaching with the indigenous traditions of Melanesia
|journal=Beyond primitivism: indigenous religious traditions and modernity
|author=Mary N. MacDonald
|publisher=Routledge
|year=2004
|isbn=041527320X}}

